# Documentation must match the product now

After any change that affects behavior, architecture, public APIs, tests, UI, auth, storage, or networking, update the relevant docs in the same work. If a doc still describes something the code no longer does, fix the doc even if the user did not ask.

Canonical docs: `README.md`, `docs/FEATURES.md`, `docs/DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md`, `docs/COMPONENT_GRAPH.md`, `docs/TESTING_CHECKLIST.md`. Skip `docs/ios27-baseline-warnings.txt` unless regenerating that baseline.

Do not invent features, TODOs-as-docs, or “coming soon”. If nothing user- or developer-facing changed, leave docs alone.

## Write the current product, not the update

Docs describe what exists today. They are not release notes, migration notes, or a history of this chat.

- Present tense. No “now”, “previously”, “we removed”, “as of this refactor”, “updated to”.
- If a capability is gone, delete every mention of it. Do not explain that it was removed.
- If a capability is new, document it as if it has always been part of the app.
- Search all docs for stale names, counts, options, routes, and file paths; remove leftovers.

```markdown
❌ BAD (changelog)
The 4-digit passcode option was removed. Users now choose 6-digit or password.

✅ GOOD (current product)
First-launch choice: 6-digit passcode or password (minimum 6 characters).
```

```markdown
❌ BAD
FileStorageManager was split; EncryptedFileStore now handles ciphertext.

✅ GOOD
FileStorageManager is the FileStorageManaging facade. EncryptedFileStore reads and writes AES-GCM files under Documents/Vault/.
```

When a list, table, graph, or checklist would still include the deleted thing, rewrite the list so that item is simply absent. Keep FEATURES.md as a current inventory, not an upgrade diary.
